course: biology
lesson: 268
title: Raptors: the real Velociraptor, claws, feathers and film mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the films, raptors are as tall as a man, scaly, and clever enough to open doors, but the real Velociraptor was about the size of a turkey and covered in feathers.
explain: Bumps on a Velociraptor arm bone, called quill knobs, show exactly where big feathers were fixed, just as on a bird's wing bone today.
explain: Its famous curved toe claw was held up off the ground and used like a hook to pin prey down, the way a hawk grips a rabbit, rather than as a knife.
explain: A real fossil shows a Velociraptor and a Protoceratops locked in a fight 75 million years ago, buried in a moment, probably when a sand dune collapsed on them.
explain: Look at a chicken's foot next time: three toes forward and a small one at the back, the same bones a raptor had, because birds are the raptors' close cousins.
fact: The real Velociraptor was about the size of a turkey and had feathers; the film raptors were based on a bigger relative, Deinonychus.
fact: Quill knobs on a Velociraptor arm bone show where large feathers were attached; small feathers leave no knobs, so a bone without knobs does not prove an animal had no feathers.
fact: Raptors held their big curved toe claw off the ground while walking and probably used it to pin down prey, like hawks today.
fact: The fighting dinosaurs fossil shows a Velociraptor and a Protoceratops buried mid-fight, probably by a collapsing sand dune, about 75 million years ago.
fact: Utahraptor was about 6 metres long and as heavy as a polar bear, and lived earlier than Velociraptor; most raptors were small.
fact: Raptors had fairly large brains for dinosaurs, like some birds, but there is no proof they were cleverer than dogs or planned attacks together.
fact: A few trackways show raptors walking together, but the evidence for pack hunting is thin, so scientists keep an open mind.
fact: Oviraptor was named «egg thief» because it was found on a nest, but later finds showed it was sitting on its own eggs like a hen.
fact: Microraptor had long feathers on its arms and legs, could glide between trees, and its feathers were shiny black like a starling's, which scientists could tell from tiny colour-holding shapes kept in the fossil feathers.
fact: Most raptors could not fly, because their arms were too short; their feathered arms helped with balance, display and covering eggs, and feathers came before flight.
fact: Young birds today flap their wings to help them run up steep slopes, and some scientists think small feathered dinosaurs did the same on the way to flight.
fact: A raptor's foot and a chicken's foot have three main toes forward and a small toe at the back, built from the same bones.
exercise: How big was the real Velociraptor? | About the size of a turkey | Taller than a man | As big as an elephant | As small as a house mouse
exercise: Which dinosaur were the film raptors really based on? | Deinonychus, a bigger relative | T. rex, scaled down to fit the set | Stegosaurus, with the plates taken off | A crocodile, standing up on two legs
exercise: What do quill knobs on a Velociraptor bone show? | Where its feathers were fixed | Where its scales were thickest | Where its teeth grew | Where its brain was
exercise: How did a raptor carry its big curved toe claw? | Held up off the ground | Dragging along the ground | Folded under its belly | On its hand
exercise: What was the big toe claw probably used for? | Pinning prey down, like a hawk | Digging tunnels underground | Climbing up steep cliffs only | Cutting and raking up grass
exercise: What does the fighting dinosaurs fossil show? | A Velociraptor and a Protoceratops buried in the middle of a fight | Two T. rex lying side by side, hugging each other as a flood buried them | A raptor sitting quietly by a nest, eating an egg it had just stolen | A raptor flying high over the trees with its feathered arms spread open
exercise: What probably buried the fighting dinosaurs? | A collapsing sand dune | A great tidal wave | Hot lava from a volcano | People long ago
exercise: How big was Utahraptor? | About 6 metres long, as heavy as a polar bear | As small as a chicken, like a little Velociraptor | As big as Argentinosaurus, the heaviest dinosaur | About 50 centimetres long, as small as a cat
exercise: How clever were raptors, as far as we know? | Fairly brainy for dinosaurs, but no proof they were cleverer than dogs | Cleverer than people, just as the films show when they plan their attacks | Clever enough to open doors, turn handles and use simple tools of stone | They had no brain at all and moved only by the instinct of their nerves
exercise: Did raptors hunt in packs? | Maybe; the evidence is thin, so scientists keep an open mind | Yes, it has been proved for every kind of raptor | No, raptors lived alone and never met each other at all | Yes, in huge packs of a hundred or more, like wild dogs
exercise: Why is the name Oviraptor, «egg thief», unfair? | It was really sitting on its own eggs | It never laid or touched eggs | It only ever ate leaves and roots | It was really a fish, not a dinosaur
exercise: What was special about Microraptor? | It had feathers on arms and legs and could glide | It had four legs, no arms and no tail at all | It could swim deep underwater like a whale | It was the biggest raptor that ever lived on Earth
exercise: What colour were Microraptor's feathers? | Shiny black, like a starling | Bright pink | Green and yellow stripes | We can never know any colour
exercise: Could most raptors fly? | No, their arms were too short for flight | Yes, all of them flew above the trees | Yes, but only at night when it was cool | No, because they had no arms at all
exercise: Which came first, feathers or flight? | Feathers came first | Flight came first | They came at exactly the same time | Neither, raptors had fur
exercise: How is a raptor's foot like a chicken's foot? | Three toes forward and a small one at the back, with the same bones | They both have five long toes, like our hands | They both have wide webbed toes for paddling through water | They are not alike at all; one has hooves and one has toes
test: «Jurassic Park raptors open doors, they were proper genius killers» What is right? | Real Velociraptors were turkey-sized and not shown to be cleverer than dogs | Yes, they were cleverer than people and planned all their attacks together | Yes, and they used keys and tools, which is why they were so deadly | No, they were dumber than worms and could not even find their own food
test: A cartoon shows a scaly green Velociraptor as tall as a man. Fix it. | A turkey-sized animal with feathers | A giant with no claws at all | A scaly animal as big as a bus | A furry animal with long rabbit ears
test: Count them: 3 raptors and 2 chickens stand in a row. How many big toe claws do the raptors have in all, one on each foot? | 6 | 3 | 12 | 10
test: What would happen if scientists found a raptor arm bone with no quill knobs? | It would not mean no feathers, since small feathers leave no knobs | It would prove at once that no raptor ever had any feathers at all | It would prove that raptors could fly, since knobs slow birds down | It would prove that the bone had really come from a fish
test: «my little bro runs down the hill flapping his arms like a bird lol» What does flapping really help young birds do? | Run up steep slopes by pushing them against the ground | Fly straight up into the sky, high enough to reach the clouds | Dig deep holes in the soil to find worms | Swim faster underwater to catch little fish
test: A museum guide says «Oviraptor stole eggs, look at its name!» What is better? | Later fossils show it was sitting on its own eggs | Yes, it stole other dinosaurs' eggs every single day | It stole only T. rex eggs, which were the biggest | It never went near any eggs, not even its own
test: Why is the fighting dinosaurs fossil so rare and special? | It catches one moment of behaviour, not just bones | It still has the dinosaurs' voices and smells inside | It shows a living dinosaur that is still breathing | It is made of solid gold instead of stone
test: «Utahraptor was tiny like Velociraptor yeah?» What is true? | No, Utahraptor was about 6 metres long | Yes, both were turkey-sized | Yes, Utahraptor was even smaller than that | No, Utahraptor was a sea monster
test: A raptor with a big toe claw is chasing a lizard. How does it use the claw? | Like a hook to pin the lizard down | Like a sword to slash from far away | As a spoon to dig | It does not use it at all
test: «did raptors hunt like wolves in a pack or what» What is the honest answer? | We are not sure; the evidence is thin | Yes, it is proved for every single raptor | No, they hunted only in the sea | Only on cold nights, never by day
test: How do scientists know the colour of Microraptor's feathers? | From tiny shapes in the fossil feathers that hold colour | From a photo that someone took of it at the time | They just guessed it from an old cartoon on the television | From the colour of the rock that the fossil lay in
exam: Identify the approximate dimensions of Velociraptor as established by its fossil remains. | Comparable to those of a turkey | Comparable to those of an adult human | Comparable to those of an elephant | Comparable to those of a mouse
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the integument of Velociraptor. | Quill knobs upon the forearm bone attest to the presence of feathers | It was covered exclusively with large overlapping scales, as in a crocodile | It was covered with a dense coat of fur | It possessed neither feathers nor scales, but bare skin
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the designation Oviraptor, signifying «egg thief», is regarded as inaccurate. | The animal was subsequently shown to be brooding its own eggs | The animal was shown never to have laid eggs | The animal was shown to have fed solely upon leaves | The animal was shown to be aquatic
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the evidence for pack hunting among the raptors? | It is limited, and the question remains open | It is conclusive in respect of all species | It is entirely absent, the animals having been solitary by proof | It derives principally from cinematic depiction
